    <fr:title text="Euclidean Topology">Euclidean Topology</fr:title>
    <fr:taxon>Definition</fr:taxon>
  </fr:frontmatter>
  <fr:mainmatter>
    <html:p>
A subset <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[S]]></fr:tex> of <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\mathbb {R}]]></fr:tex> is said to be open in the <html:strong>euclidean topology</html:strong> on <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\mathbb {R}]]></fr:tex> if <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\forall  x\in  S]]></fr:tex> there exists <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[a,b\in \mathbb {R}]]></fr:tex> with <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[a<b]]></fr:tex> st <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[x\in (a,b)\in  S]]></fr:tex>.
</html:p>
    <html:p>The dual definition (using closed sets) is also valid.</html:p>
    <html:p>
For each <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[a]]></fr:tex> and <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[b]]></fr:tex> in <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\mathbb {R}]]></fr:tex> with <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[a<b]]></fr:tex> the closed intervcal <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[[a,b]]]></fr:tex> is a closed set in the euclidean topology in <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\mathbb {R}]]></fr:tex>.
</html:p>
